      "text": "To find the perimeter of the larger triangle, we first need to find the scale factor between the two triangles. Since the longest sides are proportional, we can use the ratio of the longest sides to find the scale factor.\n\nThe longest side of the smaller triangle is 10 cm, and the longest side of the larger triangle is 25 cm. So, the scale factor is 25/10 = 5/2.\n\nNow that we know the scale factor, we can find the side lengths of the larger triangle. Since the triangles are similar, the ratio of the corresponding sides will be the same as the scale factor.\n\nThe side lengths of the smaller triangle are 8 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm. So, the side lengths of the larger triangle will be:\n\n(8 cm * 5/2) = 20 cm\n(8 cm * 5/2) = 20 cm\n(10 cm * 5/2) = 25 cm\n\nNow that we know the side lengths of the larger triangle, we can find the perimeter by adding up all the side lengths:\n\n20 cm + 20 cm + 25 cm = 65 cm\n\nSo, the perimeter of the larger triangle is 65 cm.",
